@page "/vehicle-portal"
@attribute [Authorize]
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@inject IVehiclePortalReader Portal
@inject EvInfo Car
@inject IVehicleAccountSignIn Account
@inject IVehicleTelemetry Feed
@inject TimeProvider Time

@*
    Reading the car from the manufacturer's own portal, on demand (issues #137/#139).

    A button rather than a console harness with four environment variables, because proving that a
    sign-in works and that the mapping recognises this car's fields is something an owner does — and
    an owner should not have to run `dotnet run -- vw-probe` to do it.

    Deliberately manual, and it stayed that way after #140 built the feed. Nothing polls THIS page,
    and the reading it shows is not written into IVehicleTelemetry: the dashboard's car comes from
    whatever feed is configured. Pressing the button is how the credentials and the mapping are proved
    before anything signs in unattended -- and how they are re-checked afterwards, since a feed blocked
    on a consent screen stops asking by design.

    Owner-first since #178. The page used to answer "does the mapping work at all" in three sections,
    which was the right shape while that was the open question and the wrong one once it was settled:
    the battery percentage was the fourth thing you read. It now leads with the car, in the same
    figures the dashboard's card shows, and the three diagnostic sections are one disclosure below it.
    They were not dropped -- #140 needed them in anger, and they are still the only thing that answers
    "why is this field missing" when a car starts reporting something new.
*@

<SiteTitle Page="Vehicle portal" />

@*
    Signing in to the manufacturer account (issue #170). It lives on this page rather than behind a
    settings screen because of when it is needed: volkswagen.de's cold login always emails a one-time
    code, so it can only be done while a person is here to read it -- and the moment a person wants
    the car's state is exactly that moment.

    The session it establishes is what the live source then polls on while a charge runs. Nothing
    here is required for charging: a controller with no account signed in charges exactly as before.
*@
@if (Account.IsConfigured)
{
    <h2>@Account.AccountName</h2>

    <p class="muted">
        The live source, as distinct from the portal below. Signing in here is what lets the car be
        followed while it charges; the code Volkswagen emails can only be answered by you, which is
        why this is a page and not a background job.
    </p>

    @if (_signIn.IsSignedIn)
    {
        <p class="muted"><strong>Signed in</strong> — @_signIn.Message</p>
        <div class="actions">
            <button type="button" class="secondary" id="account-signout" @onclick="SignOutAsync">Sign out</button>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        @if (_signIn.Status != VehicleSignInStatus.Unknown)
        {
            <p class="@(_signIn.Status == VehicleSignInStatus.Failed ? "error" : "muted")">@_signIn.Message</p>
        }

        @if (_signIn.WantsCode)
        {
            <div class="control">
                <label for="account-code">One-time code</label>
                <input id="account-code" type="text" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="one-time-code"
                       @bind="_code" @bind:event="oninput" />
            </div>

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="button" class="secondary" id="account-code-submit"
                        disabled="@(_busy || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_code))" @onclick="SubmitCodeAsync">
                    @(_busy ? "Checking…" : "Submit code")
                </button>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="actions">
                <button type="button" class="secondary" id="account-signin"
                        disabled="@_busy" @onclick="SignInAsync">
                    @(_busy ? "Signing in…" : "Sign in")
                </button>
            </div>
        }
    }
}


<h1>Vehicle portal</h1>

<p class="muted">
    Asks @Portal.PortalName for @CarName, once, when you press the button. <em>This press</em> runs on
    no schedule and what it shows is fed to nothing — not the dashboard's card, not any charging
    decision — so it is how you check that the credentials work and that the portal's fields are
    understood, whether or not the feed on its own clock is switched on.
</p>

@if (!Portal.IsConfigured)
{
    <p class="error">
        Not configured: needs @Portal.DescribeWhatIsMissing().
    </p>

    <p class="hint">
        Set <code>VW_BRAND</code>, <code>VW_USERNAME</code> and <code>VW_PASSWORD</code> in
        <code>.env</code> (or <code>Vehicle__DataAct__Brand</code> and friends) and restart. The
        portal also needs a <em>continuous data request</em> enabling by hand before it delivers
        anything — see <code>docs/VW_PORTAL_SETUP.md</code>.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <div class="actions">
        <button type="button" class="secondary" id="portal-read" disabled="@_busy" @onclick="ReadAsync">
            @(_busy ? "Reading…" : "Read the car now")
        </button>
    </div>

    <p class="hint">
        Each press signs in afresh. That is fine by hand and is why this is not a poll: repeatedly
        replaying a password at a real identity provider is how accounts get locked.
    </p>
}

@if (_reading is { Succeeded: false } failed)
{
    <p class="error">
        <strong>@failed.FailureKind</strong> — @failed.Message
    </p>

    <p class="hint">
        @(failed.IsWorthRetrying
            ? "Worth pressing again — this one is expected and usually clears itself."
            : "Pressing again will not help. This one needs something changed, or a browser.")
    </p>
}
else if (_reading is { Succeeded: true, State: { } state })
{
    @*
        The car, in the dashboard card's own figures and its own component (#178) — battery, range,
        reading age, charge state, plug state — plus the two the portal carries that the card cannot:
        the car's own charge limit, and its own estimate of how much longer it needs.

        Same Stat component and the same formatters, deliberately: the reason to look at this page
        beside the dashboard is to compare them, and a difference in rounding would read as a
        difference in the reading.
    *@
    <h2>@CarName</h2>

    <section class="stat-grid" id="car">
        <Stat Label="Car battery" Value="@VehicleFormatting.Percent(state.SocPercent)"
              Hint="The car's own state of charge, as the portal answered this press. Not the home battery." />
        <Stat Label="Car range" Value="@VehicleFormatting.Km(state.RangeKm)"
              Hint="What the car reckons is left in it, off its own recent consumption. Nothing here plans on it; it is the figure that says whether the charge you are about to ask for is enough." />
        <Stat Label="Car reading age" Value="@Age(state)"
              Hint="How long ago the car itself produced this reading — not how long ago you pressed the button. Hours old is normal for a parked car." />
        <Stat Label="Car charge state" Value="@state.ChargeState.ToString()"
              Hint="What the car says it is doing: Idle, Charging, Complete, or Unknown when it doesn't report." />
        <Stat Label="Car plug state" Value="@state.PlugState.ToString()"
              Hint="Whether the car says a cable is in its socket. A separate reading from the charger's own status, and a much slower one." />
        <Stat Label="Target SOC" Value="@VehicleFormatting.Percent(_reading.TargetSocPercent)"
              Hint="The car's own charge limit, set in the car or its app. The portal carries it and the dashboard's card cannot: no plan reads it yet (#101). A dash means this bundle did not carry it." />
        <Stat Label="Time left" Value="@VehicleFormatting.ChargeTime(state)"
              Hint="The car's own estimate of how much longer it needs, not one of ours. A dash means the portal did not carry it — which is itself a finding." />
    </section>

    <p class="muted table-note">
        The capture time is the <em>car's</em>, not this moment's. A parked car reporting hours ago is
        normal and not a fault — its state of charge does not drift.
    </p>

    @*
        This press against what the feed is holding (#178).

        They are different sessions, asking at different moments, and the point of saying so out loud
        is that a small disagreement here is the expected outcome rather than evidence of a fault.
        A large or persistent one is a real finding, and the feeds page is where it gets counted
        rather than eyeballed.
    *@
    @if (Feed.GetCurrentState() is { } held)
    {
        <p class="muted" id="against-the-feed">
            The dashboard's card is showing @VehicleFormatting.Percent(held.SocPercent) from
            @(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(held.SourceId) ? "the feed" : held.SourceId), captured
            @Age(held) ago@(Difference(state, held)). That is a different session asking at a different
            moment, so a difference is not by itself a fault —
            <a href="/vehicle-feeds">the feeds page</a> is where a persistent one is counted.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="muted" id="against-the-feed">
            Nothing has reached the dashboard's card yet, so there is nothing to hold this against.
            That is what an install with no feed switched on looks like, and it is not a fault: this
            press is the whole reading.
        </p>
    }
}

@*
    Everything the page used to lead with, one disclosure down (#178).

    Collapsed when the read worked, because then it is the answer to a question nobody is asking.
    Open when it did not, because then it is the answer to the only question there is: a bundle in
    which nothing matched is a failure whose field list IS the diagnosis, and hiding it behind a
    click would be a page reporting "unusable data" while holding the answer in its hand.
*@
@if (_reading is not null && HasDiagnostics)
{
    <details class="diagnostics" id="diagnostics" open="@(!_reading.Succeeded)">
        <summary>Diagnostics</summary>

        <p class="muted">
            What the portal delivered and how much of it this build understands. This is what answers
            <em>why is that field a dash</em> — worth opening when a figure above is missing, and
            worth ignoring when none is.
        </p>

        @*
            The delivery itself, shown whether the mapping succeeded or not: how many snapshots arrived
            and what they span is how "this quarter-hour said nothing" is told apart from "the portal
            handed us one report type and the battery is in another delivery". On a failure it is half
            the diagnosis.
        *@
        @if (_reading is { SnapshotCount: > 0 })
        {
            <h3>The delivery</h3>

            <dl class="facts">
                <dt>Vehicle</dt>
                <dd>@_reading.Vehicle</dd>

                <dt>Snapshots</dt>
                <dd>
                    @_reading.SnapshotCount
                    @if (_reading.OldestSnapshot is { } oldest && _reading.NewestSnapshot is { } newest)
                    {
                        @* A merged read reaches back as far as it has to, and the reference car's deliveries
                           are not a tidy quarter-hour apart -- the first live merge spanned most of a day. As
                           bare clock times that read as "16:30 - 10:29", which looks like a fault rather than
                           a span. The date appears exactly when the two ends disagree about it. *@
                        <span class="muted"> (@Span(oldest, newest))</span>
                    }
                </dd>

                @* Repeated from the card above rather than moved out of it: on a failed read there is
                   no card, and this is then the only place the bundle's own figures can appear. *@
                <dt>Target SOC</dt>
                <dd>@VehicleFormatting.Percent(_reading.TargetSocPercent)</dd>

                <dt>Odometer</dt>
                <dd>@VehicleFormatting.Km(_reading.OdometerKm)</dd>
            </dl>

            <p class="muted table-note">
                The odometer is carried but nothing reads it. Target SOC is the one that would let a
                plan refuse an impossible battery target, deferred on
                <a href="https://github.com/mpospisil/gleanvolt/issues/101">#101</a>.
            </p>
        }

        @*
            What the vocabulary DID match, and what each of those fields actually said.

            The half that was missing the first time this page was needed in anger. A name absent from
            the unrecognised list below means one of two opposite things -- the bundle never carried
            it, or it carried it and the value was a sentinel that filtering threw away -- and those
            want opposite fixes: a name to add, against a car that reports the field empty. Values are
            shown raw, quoted and untouched, because "" and "invalid" are exactly what this exists to
            reveal.
        *@
        @if (_reading.Matched.Count > 0)
        {
            <h3>@_reading.Matched.Count field(s) this build recognised</h3>

            <p class="muted">
                Each with the moment the <em>car</em> reported it. A read merges several deliveries, so
                two figures here can be hours apart — and comparing them without that is how a coarse
                reading and a stale one get mistaken for each other.
            </p>

            @* .fields, not .facts: the name is the data here, and a fifty-character dotted path overruns a
               column sized to fit its own content. *@
            <dl class="fields">
                @foreach (var (field, reading) in _reading.Matched.OrderByDescending(entry => entry.Value.At))
                {
                    <dt><code>@Breakable(field)</code></dt>
                    <dd>
                        @(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(reading.Value)
                            ? (MarkupString)"(empty)"
                            : Breakable($"\"{reading.Value}\""))
                        <span class="muted">@reading.At.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm")</span>
                    </dd>
                }
            </dl>
        }
        else if (_reading.Succeeded || _reading.Unmapped.Count > 0)
        {
            <p class="muted">
                Not one field in the bundle is in the vocabulary at all — so this is a set of names to
                add rather than a car reporting empty values.
            </p>
        }

        @if (_reading.Notes.Count > 0)
        {
            <h3>What the bundle held that never became a reading</h3>

            @foreach (var note in _reading.Notes)
            {
                <p class="muted">@note</p>
            }

            @if (_reading.Dropped.Count > 0)
            {
                <ul class="sessions">
                    @foreach (var field in _reading.Dropped)
                    {
                        <li><code>@field</code></li>
                    }
                </ul>
            }
        }

        @*
            The unrecognised names, shown whether the read succeeded or not.

            They used to hang off the success branch, which put them exactly where they were least
            needed: a bundle in which *nothing* matched is a failure, and that is precisely the case
            where this list is the whole answer.
        *@
        @if (_reading.Unmapped.Count > 0)
        {
            <h3>@_reading.Unmapped.Count field(s) nothing here reads</h3>

            <p class="muted">
                If a value above is missing, its real name is probably in this list — the portal's
                vocabulary was written down from VW's data dictionary rather than from your car, so
                this is expected on a first run rather than a fault. Adding the right names to
                <code>VwGroupFieldNames</code> is the fix; pasting this list into an issue is enough
                for somebody else to do it.
            </p>

            <ul class="sessions">
                @foreach (var field in _reading.Unmapped)
                {
                    <li><code>@field</code></li>
                }
            </ul>
        }
        else if (_reading.Succeeded)
        {
            <p class="muted">Every field in the bundle was recognised.</p>
        }
    </details>
}

@code {
    private VehicleSignInState _signIn = VehicleSignInState.Unknown;
    private string _code = string.Empty;

    protected override void OnInitialized() => _signIn = Account.State;

    /// <summary>
    /// Starts a sign-in. Never called on render: opening a page must not replay a password, and a
    /// refresh must not email the owner a code they did not ask for.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task SignInAsync()
    {
        _busy = true;
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            _signIn = await Account.SignInAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    private async Task SubmitCodeAsync()
    {
        _busy = true;
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            _signIn = await Account.SubmitCodeAsync(_code);

            if (_signIn.IsSignedIn)
            {
                _code = string.Empty;
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    private Task SignOutAsync()
    {
        Account.SignOut();
        _signIn = Account.State;
        _code = string.Empty;
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private VehiclePortalReading? _reading;
    private bool _busy;

    /// <summary>The owner's word for the car, falling back to something that reads as a sentence.</summary>
    private string CarName => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Car.Name) ? "the car" : Car.Name;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the disclosure has anything in it at all — measured on content, never on success.
    ///
    /// <para>A reading that succeeded while carrying no snapshots, no matched fields and nothing
    /// unrecognised is not reachable from the real reader (a success is a bundle something mapped out
    /// of), but it is trivially constructible, and the sections inside would then answer with
    /// "not one field is in the vocabulary" and "every field was recognised" at once. Nothing to
    /// show is a reason for no lid rather than for an empty one.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool HasDiagnostics =>
        _reading is { SnapshotCount: > 0 }
        || _reading?.Matched.Count > 0
        || _reading?.Unmapped.Count > 0
        || _reading?.Notes.Count > 0;

    private async Task ReadAsync()
    {
        _busy = true;
        _reading = null;
        StateHasChanged();

        try
        {
            // The reader does not throw for an expected failure -- a refused password, a consent
            // screen, a portal with nothing to give all come back as an unsuccessful reading. Only a
            // genuine bug would escape, and swallowing that here would hide it.
            _reading = await Portal.ReadAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    private string Age(VehicleState state) => VehicleFormatting.Age(state, Time.GetUtcNow());

    /// <summary>
    /// How far this press and the feed's held reading are apart, as a clause to hang off the end of
    /// the sentence. Empty when there is nothing to compare — one of the two carrying no state of
    /// charge is a fact about the sources, not a disagreement between them.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Difference(VehicleState pressed, VehicleState held)
    {
        if (pressed.SocPercent is not { } here || held.SocPercent is not { } there)
        {
            return string.Empty;
        }

        // Compared at the precision both are displayed at. Two readings that print as 64% must not be
        // described as differing, whatever their third decimal places are doing.
        var gap = Math.Round(here) - Math.Round(there);

        if (gap == 0)
        {
            return " — the same state of charge";
        }

        var size = Math.Abs(gap);
        return $" — {size:F0} point{(size == 1 ? "" : "s")} {(gap > 0 ? "below" : "above")} this press";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A portal field name with break opportunities marked after its separators.
    ///
    /// <para>These names run to fifty characters of dotted path and the values to
    /// <c>CHARGE_STATE_NOT_READY_FOR_CHARGING</c>. Left to itself a browser breaks such a token at
    /// whatever character reaches the edge — <c>current_charge_st / ate</c> — which is legible only
    /// just. A <c>&lt;wbr&gt;</c> after each dot and underscore gives it the joints to break at
    /// instead, and it falls back to breaking anywhere when even a segment does not fit.</para>
    ///
    /// <para>Every segment is HTML-encoded on the way through: these strings come from the portal, and
    /// this is the one place in the page that emits markup rather than text.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private static MarkupString Breakable(string text)
    {
        var builder = new System.Text.StringBuilder(text.Length + 32);

        foreach (var character in text)
        {
            builder.Append(System.Net.WebUtility.HtmlEncode(character.ToString()));

            if (character is '.' or '_')
            {
                builder.Append("<wbr>");
            }
        }

        return (MarkupString)builder.ToString();
    }

    private static string Span(DateTimeOffset oldest, DateTimeOffset newest) =>
        oldest.Date == newest.Date
            ? $"{oldest:HH:mm} – {newest:HH:mm}"
            : $"{oldest:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm} – {newest:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm}";
}
